def tuple_intersection(tuple_list, other_tuple_list):
    """
    Returns a set of tuples that are common to both input lists,
    ignoring the order of elements within each tuple.
    """
    # Convert each tuple to a frozenset to make it hashable and order-independent
    def to_frozenset(t):
        return frozenset(t)

    # Create sets of frozensets for both lists
    set1 = {to_frozenset(t) for t in tuple_list}
    set2 = {to_frozenset(t) for t in other_tuple_list}

    # Find the intersection
    common = set1 & set2

    # Convert back to tuples and return as a set
    return {tuple(item) for item in common}
```

This function works by converting each tuple into a `frozenset`, which is an unordered, hashable collection. This allows us to compare tuples regardless of element order. We then find the intersection of these sets and convert the result back into a set of tuples.

